<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matanzas Inlet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a> Inlet is a channel linking the Matanzas River to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, about 23 km south of St Augustine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. In September and October 1565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault">Jean Ribault</a> and several hundred shipwrecked French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> executed there, which gave the inlet its name, Spanish for 'slaughters'. Fort Matanzas, built by the Spanish in 1740–1742, guards it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matanzas-inlet.jpg" alt="Matanzas Inlet" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image courtesy of Janet Zimmerman at Florida Inland Navigation District · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matanzas_Inlet_Aerial_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
